// The boss journal: the Hollow King, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).hollowking = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "A king who follows whoever holds him, plain enough, but his whole hall answers for him: bells, knights, coffins. More than one man's footing can cover. I told the line we had faced worse. That was a lie, and good for morale. Gave ground.",
    gloomsworn: "He is a king who is followed rather than fought: bells, knights and coffins, all at his word. I walked into the Barrow and nothing in it was surprised to see me. It is a strange thing, to be expected by the dead.",
    thresher: "Barrows I know: cold earth, and what goes in does not always stay down. All he did came back off the walls a beat later, like an echo in a cellar. The old King inclined his crown to me. I think it was courtesy.",
    bloodreaver: "The old bones hit like there's still meat on them! A greatsword that leaves the wound open and weeping, and I haven't bled like that since the north. I like him already. Don't tell the others. They'll want a turn.",
    runeguard: "A court has procedure, and the Barrow King keeps one: he calls, and a beat later the hall answers, and every sign he gives returns to be read again. I carried my best folio into the tomb and read the walls like a text. Not foreseen; now it is.",
    gorgemaw: "A cold table, no fire, no salt, every dish served twice, once by the King and again by his guests. A dead man's kitchen. After Volkar's austerity this is a famine. I ate nothing and learned a great deal. I will be back for every course.",
    oathbroker: "A king holds court in his own tomb and reads out charges against the living, and I have never argued before a dead judge. His terms are strict, and he collects in full. I have requested a right of reply. So noted. I am waiting.",
    ironpalm: "The King calls, and his hall answers. Bells, knights, coffins: every word said twice. I listened too little and answered too late. Silence is also a sentence, and I had not learned to read it. Breathe.",
    ranger: "Long hall, bad light, after Volkar's open summit. The quarry walks after our knight, and whatever it does comes back off the walls a beat later. Spent the night shooting at echoes. No line yet.",
    shadowblade: "The mark keeps guards who remember everything, and an eye no one should meet. A whole court watching, and still nobody watching me. I find I am almost offended. Almost.",
    pyromancer: "A cold hall, dead braziers, and a king who GLARES! He hates being looked at, and I hate not being looked at, so one of us will have to give way, and it won't be my eyebrows. Wonderful acoustics. I told him so.",
    thermist: "Cold. All of him. The hall, the light, the knights. Then the bells rang and I ran hot, to the end, and the wrong way, twice. After Volkar's weather this is a larder. Readings to follow, once my teeth stop.",
    driftblade: "A long dark hall to run through, knights along the walls and coffins in the bays, and a king you must not look at, which means turning on the wing, and I can do that, mostly. First stone floor I have ever wished was grass.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man refuses to stay dead, and has brought his own chorus: knights, bells, coffins. Enormous staging; the company badly under-rehearsed. (Curtain, early. The Doll was very brave and rather pale.)",
    cleric: "A poor king who would not lie down, in a hall full of people who never got to. He passes sentences, and the light is the only appeal. I said a prayer for him on the stairs, and I think he heard. Dawn keep us; we shall need it.",
    grovekeeper: "The old king is winter come indoors: slow, cold and certain, and everything he does comes round again like a season. My lot were caught by the frost. I had brought no coat. Foolish, in an old woman.",
    chronomancer: "His Majesty does everything twice: once himself, and again through his court, a beat later. I thought I had heard that echo before. I had not, which is the odd part. I felt cheated of a memory.",
    alembist: "Specimen: one dead king, cold, very much awake. Symptoms: more than I could label in a night. Two lanterns broken, neither deliberate, which stings. The shelves will need restocking. Also my nerves, which are not kept on a shelf.",
    threadweaver: "His lordship keeps a hall full of loose threads: knights, bells, coffins, lanterns. My stitches wandered all over it. The sloppiest hem I have ever seen, and it was mine. Needle clenched in my teeth the whole night.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "The King went down and stayed down. At the last lantern I counted heads, every one on their feet. Something in my footing is different tonight, steadier, as though the floor had agreed to it. I wish the sergeant had seen it. Held.",
    gloomsworn: "The King is down and stays down. We carried the dark through to the last lantern. Something in me is quieter tonight, as if an old entry had been struck from a ledger. I stood a while in the doorway. The watch is not over. It is only different.",
    thresher: "I laid him back down myself and set the stone gently. Something in me turned over down there too, like a cold thing that has waited out a long winter. Not yet. A barrow never rests, you know.",
    bloodreaver: "The old bones fell, and every wound he gave me is still warm. Best fight of my life. Something woke in me down there and has not gone back to sleep. It's hungry. Splendid. Now there are two of us.",
    runeguard: "The Barrow King is judged at last. I read every sign in his hall, and tonight I find new characters in myself that I did not inscribe. As foreseen? Not quite. I am delighted to be wrong.",
    gorgemaw: "The old cook has served his last course and left the table for good. I ate the whole night through, and still a new hunger woke in me, one I cannot name. It does not want bread. It does not want meat. I am interested.",
    oathbroker: "The Crown's account is closed and the court dismissed. Yet I find a clause in myself I do not remember signing, in a hand I almost recognise. Countersigned, all the same. One reads before signing. One may also read after.",
    ironpalm: "The King spoke his last word, and we answered it. Since then something in me speaks with a voice I have not heard before. I listen. I do not answer yet. Breathe.",
    ranger: "The old stag's down, and the moon still up. Something sharper in my eye since. Saw a moth on the far wall without looking for it. Clean line.",
    shadowblade: "The mark fell, and for once I looked it in the face. Something in my shadow moves differently now, a half-step ahead of me. It never saw me. I am no longer sure I see me.",
    pyromancer: "He fell! The moon still up, the hall still dark, and me the brightest thing in it. Something new is smouldering in me, and I adore it, and I am not going to be modest about it. Eyebrows: negotiable.",
    thermist: "Cold all night. Then he fell, and I ran hot with it. Now there is a third thing in me, neither hot nor cold, waiting like a held breath. Reading: unknown. Thrilled. Perhaps the middle has moved.",
    driftblade: "He fell, the hall went quiet, and I kept moving anyway, out through the doors and round the barrow, and there is something new in the wind now, like a road I haven't taken yet and somehow already like.",
    marionettist: "Final curtain for His Majesty, and the whole company standing at the end. My Doll bowed without being asked, which is new, and I felt it in my own wrists. (Applause. Real applause. Mine, too.)",
    cleric: "The King is at rest, and every one of us walked out into the dawn. Something in the light answers me differently now, as though it knew my name and had only just remembered to use it. Thank the light.",
    grovekeeper: "The long winter is over, and my lot all came through it. Something took root in me down there; I can feel it waiting for spring, the way a bulb waits. I shall not tell it to hurry.",
    chronomancer: "We beat him before the moon set, and I did not have to undo a thing. That is new. Something in me already knows what comes next, and has not told me yet. Rude. I will have found out.",
    alembist: "Result: one king, deceased, permanently. Shelves empty, every patient alive. A new reagent has appeared in me that I certainly did not brew, and it is warm. Label: unknown. Do not shake.",
    threadweaver: "His lordship is unpicked for good and the whole hem came home. There is a new thread in me now, in a colour I don't recognise, and I did not spin it. Needle down. Pulled it tight regardless.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Barrow Knell': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The bell tolled and reached every one of us. Nothing to block, nothing to stand behind, so I stood near the healer and let the line close round me. My shield was no use. I held it up anyway, for the look of the thing.",
      gloomsworn: "The bell tolled and it reached every one of us. There was nothing to stand behind, so I kept us bunched by our healer and we took it upright. A bell is only a slow knock on a familiar door.",
      thresher: "The bell comes due for every one of us at once, and no one is spared. I made sure I was whole before it tolled and kept at my work right through it. Tolling is only a slow way of saying farewell.",
      bloodreaver: "The bell rang right through my ribs. Barely a scratch, shameful, but the others feel it, so I stayed by the healers and let them top the rest up before it tolled. Somebody has to be considerate.",
      runeguard: "Folio the first: he lifts his head, and the bells draw breath. Nobody escapes the toll, so each of us must be whole before it sounds. I waited for the first note with my pen raised. It arrived on time. I felt vindicated and slightly ill.",
      gorgemaw: "The bell is a course served to the whole table at once, and I cannot eat it for anyone. So we sat close, came to it well fed, and swallowed it together. Thin broth, and cold, but every bowl was full. I counted.",
      oathbroker: "The Knell is a levy on every one of the undersigned, payable in full; no clause excuses anyone. The only provision in our favour is to arrive already mended. We did. The King, I note, issued no receipt.",
      ironpalm: "The bell spoke to all of us at once. There is no answering it, only hearing it. We came to it whole and let it pass through us. The hall rang long after. I let it.",
      ranger: "Bell tolled. Reached everyone, no cover from it. Mended before, kept drawing through it. Not a sound I'll miss.",
      shadowblade: "The bell found everyone, even me, which was unforgivably rude. Nowhere to hide from a sound. I made sure I was whole when it tolled, went back to work, and gave the King my sternest glare.",
      pyromancer: "The bell rang so loud my hair stood on end! Nothing to dodge, so I made sure I wasn't already half scorched when it came and kept casting through it. My hat rang for an hour. Very flattering to the hat.",
      thermist: "Toll through everyone. Fact. Be whole before it. Then it hit and I went off like a kettle, rattled, still swinging, teeth buzzing like a tuning fork. Reading: B flat. Probably. Ask me when the ringing stops.",
      driftblade: "The bell rang through the whole hall and there is no slipping through it or round it, nowhere to go at all, which offends me, so I came to it whole and kept moving through the sound as if it were wind.",
      marionettist: "The great bell tolled through the whole house, stalls and wings alike. No exits for anyone; the cast came to it whole and played on. (The Doll did not flinch. I flinched on her behalf.)",
      cleric: "The bell tolls for every one of us. I mended everyone before it came, not after, and laid a little grace over my friends as he raised his head. It is kinder to arrive whole.",
      grovekeeper: "The bell comes like hail, dear: no cover, all my lot at once. So the mending was growing in everyone before it tolled. It is a poor farmer who waits for the storm to start. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The toll lands a beat after he lifts his head. I had everyone mended in the moment before; next time, which was last time, I shall start a little earlier. A good bell, on reflection. Very punctual.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the bell, felt by everyone, nowhere to hide. Remedy: the whole party whole before it tolls, the big brew poured just ahead. Front shelf, please. And I shall stop the cork squeaking; it spoiled the effect.",
      threadweaver: "The bell pulled on every thread at once. Nothing to dodge, so I had the whole party mended before it tolled and drew everyone in close. Thread that has been tugged in company holds.",
    } },
    'Sceptre Sweep': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He sweeps the sceptre across all the ground before him, and he faces me. His feet set as he winds it, and a man with set feet cannot turn. I stepped round to his flank, which is not my way; a shield is meant to face. The line was already behind him.",
      gloomsworn: "The sceptre cuts a wide arc before him, always toward me. I watched his feet stop; once they stop he cannot turn. I slipped past his shoulder, and the others had already taken the space behind him. Let him sweep an empty room.",
      thresher: "His feet set, and the sceptre sweeps across the whole front of him like a scythe. I went round to his back before it came in; once he has begun he cannot turn. A fine arc, I will grant him. I know a scythe when I see one.",
      bloodreaver: "The sceptre swept the whole front of the room, and I was in front, of course. He can't turn once he winds it, so I stepped round his side and let it whistle past. Not worth the scar. A dull crack, that. Disappointing.",
      runeguard: "Marginal note: sceptre drawn back, feet set; once begun he will not turn. So the ground before him is left entirely, for his side or his back, and returned to once it has passed. I felt very clever. The sceptre had not read the note.",
      gorgemaw: "The sweep is laid across the whole front of the table, and the King cannot turn the platter once he lifts it. The others dine at his back; some nights I bite into it myself. Generous, and bland, like a state banquet.",
      oathbroker: "The terms of the Sweep: all ground before him is liable, and once he draws the sceptre he may not amend his facing. The undersigned settled behind him, where the court has no jurisdiction.",
      ironpalm: "He drew the sceptre back and his feet set. A long word, for whatever stands before him. I stepped to his side and the word went past. Then I answered, from where he could not turn.",
      ranger: "Sweep covers the whole front of it, wall to wall. Feet lock as it winds. Kept to the back quarter and drew from there. It swept a great deal of empty floor. Funny to watch.",
      shadowblade: "The King swept the whole front of the hall, which concerned me not at all; I live behind him. The others learned to join me there, one by one, rather clumsily. Nobody saw any of us.",
      pyromancer: "He swept the whole front of the hall with that sceptre and it glowed all the way to the far wall! I moved behind him, sulking, and finished the spell from there. Behind a king. My mother would weep.",
      thermist: "Wide arc, front only. Feet set; he won't turn. Go behind. I went, quick and hot, and came out the far side still burning. Hypothesis confirmed: he rotates like a cathedral. Terrible waltz partner.",
      driftblade: "He plants his feet and sweeps the whole front of the hall, so I went round him, through the gap at his side and out the back, and he couldn't turn to follow, and I thought, a king who can only look one way, how sad.",
      marionettist: "His Majesty sweeps the entire front of the stage and cannot turn once he begins. The cast exits upstage, behind him; my Doll was there already. (A rare clean scene. Reviewers noted the Doll.)",
      cleric: "He swept the whole hall before him, and our young archer stood at the wrong end of it. Behind him, child, always behind; he cannot turn once he begins. She lived. I have told her three times since.",
      grovekeeper: "The sweep comes on like a gale across the front of the field, and nothing standing there sees the season out. Once his feet root he can't turn, so my lot shelter behind him like cattle behind a hedge.",
      chronomancer: "He sets his feet, then sweeps the whole front of the hall. Behind him, that bar is quiet. I was standing there already, I think. Not the first time I have hidden behind a king.",
      alembist: "The sceptre goes across the whole front of the room, and his feet fix once he winds it. Remedy: stand at his back. Cheap, effective, no brewing required. I had the vial out anyway. Put it away. Sheepish.",
      threadweaver: "He swept the whole front of the hall, and one of my stitches was snagged right in it. Behind him, everyone; he can't turn once he starts. Mind which end of a king you stand at.",
    } },
    'Royal Gaze': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "I was trained to face the enemy. He punishes it. When his eyes lit I turned my back on him in the last instant and turned again after. Every drill I ever learned screamed against it. I did it, and so did the line.",
      gloomsworn: "His eyes lit, and I did what a knight is taught never to do: showed him my back for a breath, then turned again. Looking at him is the thing that hurts. I have been hurt by looking at worse, and called it grief.",
      thresher: "When his eyes kindle I turn my back on him and wait the beat out. Meet that look and you stand rooted until it lets you go. Then round again for the cut.",
      bloodreaver: "Looked the King in the eye once. It froze me where I stood and hurt more than it should, and the second time hurt worse. Now I turn my back when his eyes kindle. Shameful, but sensible. Never repeat that.",
      runeguard: "His eyes kindle beneath the crown. Look elsewhere when it lands, even mid-step; whoever meets his gaze is held fast and marked, and a second meeting is crueller. I wrote this in the margin twice, and underlined it for a successor with poor eyesight.",
      gorgemaw: "His stare is the one dish I will not touch. Whoever meets his eyes is pinned to their chair, and served worse the second time. So I turned my back on him, rudely, like a man refusing a bad oyster. Sent it back.",
      oathbroker: "The Gaze binds whoever meets it, and a second meeting costs more. It often lands as a knight swings, so one must dodge facing away from him. For once, avoiding the counterparty's eye is sound practice.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke with his eyes. Some words should not be answered. I turned away until it passed, then turned back to him. A good listener also knows when to look at the floor.",
      ranger: "Eyes light. Don't look. Back turned, waited the beat, spun round and drew. Meet the stare, stay pinned. Learned that the cheap way, watching the knight.",
      shadowblade: "The mark wishes to be looked at. I never look at a mark from the front anyway; when his eyes lit I had my back turned already. Those who stared were nailed to the floor like moths.",
      pyromancer: "He wanted my full attention! I gave him my back instead, which he deserved, and the stare slid right off me. The archer looked, and stood frozen like a statue. Rather fetching. Not on purpose, I assume.",
      thermist: "Eyes kindle. Turn away. Rule. I looked once, hot and curious, and froze stiff where I stood, mid-thought, mid-sentence, mid-sneeze. After that I kept my back to him and my head level.",
      driftblade: "His stare pins you to the floor, which is the worst thing there is, so when his eyes lit I kept going with my back to him, round the hall the whole way, half looking over my shoulder at nothing.",
      marionettist: "The star demands to be looked at, and whoever obliges is frozen mid-line. The cast turns its back on cue. My Doll too; she has no eyes to speak of, but I turn her anyway. (Exit, facing away. Dignified.)",
      cleric: "Our brave knight met the King's eyes and stood frozen, and it hurt him; the second time, worse. Turn away when his eyes light, all of you. I watch the wall now, and pray, and it is a lovely wall.",
      grovekeeper: "His stare comes on like a killing frost, and whatever faces it stiffens. My lot learned to turn their backs, like sheep turning from the wind, then turn round again after.",
      chronomancer: "His eyes light, and a moment later whoever is looking at him is held. Looked once, held; looked twice, worse. I turn away on the cue now. Last time I knew that already, and looked anyway. Fascinating.",
      alembist: "Symptoms: frozen where you stand, worse on a second dose. Remedy: your back to him as it lands, even mid-step. It likes to arrive while the knights are swinging. (Do not look to check. I checked.)",
      threadweaver: "Whoever looks him in the eye is pinned like cloth under a needle, and it pricks worse the second time. My stitches turn their backs on him now, like sensible sheep.",
    } },
    'Royal Audience': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "His crown blazed, and this time he wanted to be faced. I always face him; for once that was the whole duty. The line turned to him with me, square, chins up, nobody caught looking away. It felt like a parade. I enjoyed it more than I will say.",
      gloomsworn: "This time the crown blazed, and he wished to be seen. For once, facing the enemy was the entire duty. We all stood turned to the throne, and no one was caught looking away. A courtesy I had nearly forgotten how to pay.",
      thresher: "Different light: the crown blazes, not the eyes, and this time I face him square. Turn your back on that one and it roots you all the same. Courteous of him, wanting to be looked at.",
      bloodreaver: "When the crown blazes, look the King right in the face. Easy! The ones who showed him their backs were frozen and hurt. I grinned at him the whole time. He didn't grin back. No sense of humour, the dead.",
      runeguard: "The crown blazes, not the eyes, and the answer is the Gaze reversed: face him squarely as it lands. A careless reader confuses the two signs; I nearly did. Note to successor: do not confuse them. I mean it.",
      gorgemaw: "The King's crown blazed, and he demanded every guest face the head of the table. Turn your back on this host and you are pinned to your chair. I faced him with relish. The one meal where eye contact is the main course.",
      oathbroker: "The Audience reverses the terms of the Gaze: he must be faced as it lands. The difficulty is telling the crown from the eyes. Those who turned away were held in contempt, quite literally.",
      ironpalm: "His crown blazed. This time he wished to be heard. I faced him and listened. Those who turned away were held. There is a time to look away and a time to look, and the difference is a light on his head.",
      ranger: "Crown burns, not eyes: face it. Back to it and you're pinned. Watched the head, turned square. Opposite of last time. Annoying.",
      shadowblade: "The crown blazed and the mark demanded to be faced. Showing him my back, my usual habit, earns a freezing. So I looked him full in the face, just this once, and hated every instant. I remain unseen in spirit.",
      pyromancer: "His crown blazed the colour of trouble, and for once he wanted us looking! Finally, a man of taste. I faced him and kept casting. Those who turned away froze stiff. Applause for the crown, though; it competes with me.",
      thermist: "Crown blazes: face him. Eyes kindle: look away. Simple. I mixed them up once, boiled with embarrassment, and froze solid with my back to him. Mnemonic: crown, chin up. Eyes, eyes down.",
      driftblade: "The crown blazed and he wanted every face on him, so I circled him with my eyes on him the whole way, sideways through the hall, which is harder than it sounds and rather fun.",
      marionettist: "When the crown blazes, the monarch demands the house face the stage. Every player turns to him; backs to the star are frozen. (The Doll faced him beautifully. She has a gift for it.)",
      cleric: "When his crown blazes he must be faced; when his eyes light, he must not. Our poor archer confused them and stood frozen. I faced him and kept praying. Gently, child. Gently.",
      grovekeeper: "The crown blazes like a summer sun, and this time my lot turn their faces to it like flowers. Back to it and you're frozen. Different weather, different answer, dear. Never trust a sky that doesn't change.",
      chronomancer: "Crown, not eyes: at that signal he must be faced. I turned to him a moment early, I'm sure. I did. Those with their backs to him were held. I confess I enjoyed being right about the order.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the blazing crown. Symptoms: freezing, but only for those with their backs to him. Remedy: face him as it lands. Shelve well apart from the Gaze; the labels look alike. (I swapped them. Once. Educational.)",
      threadweaver: "His crown blazed, and every stitch had to face him. One turned away and was pinned fast. A sloppy seam, and I told her so. Chin up, all of you, needle ready.",
    } },
    'Grave Cleave': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The greatsword came down on me alone, and it leaves the wound open after. Shield up in the last instant, the line kept clear of the ground in front of him, and I bled quietly. Complaining about a wound is for the infirmary. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The great sword fell on me alone, and the cut it leaves does not close. I pulled the dark in at the last breath and bled where I stood. The others kept out of the space in front of him. I have worn open wounds before. They do not improve.",
      thresher: "The greatsword comes due for our knight alone, so I keep out of the ground before him and work from his side. The wound goes on weeping after. Not mine to carry, that one.",
      bloodreaver: "The greatsword! A deep one, and it keeps bleeding long after. Lovely. I braced, let it land, then drank it back while it was still running. Hold still, old bones, and let me say thank you.",
      runeguard: "The greatsword lifts over me alone. A ward a breath before; the others well clear of the ground before him; our healers ready for the bleeding that follows, since the wound stays open. Even a sword has a footnote.",
      gorgemaw: "The greatsword is served to me alone, a heavy cut with a sauce that keeps running long after. I opened wide just before it, kept the others off my plate, and chewed. Rare, and good. Deeply unhygienic.",
      oathbroker: "The Cleave is payable by whoever stands before him, and the bleeding that follows compounds. I accepted delivery behind every protection I had, the undersigned well clear. Interest accrues until the healers settle it.",
      ironpalm: "The greatsword came for me. I met it at its last instant with everything raised. The wound kept speaking after; I let it, and answered him. I do not argue with a cut.",
      ranger: "Big blade, meant for our knight. Nobody else in front of it. Watched from the flank. Wound kept bleeding after. Does not look like a fun job.",
      shadowblade: "The greatsword came down on our knight, as it should. The front of the mark is no place for anyone else, and the bleeding after is a vulgar business. I stayed at his back and let our knight deal with his manners.",
      pyromancer: "The greatsword came down on our knight with a terrible gleam! None of my business, provided I'm nowhere in front of him. I kept well clear. Hair intact. Pride: intact. Knight: bleeding, but grumbling.",
      thermist: "Greatsword, for our knight. Stay clear of his front. Then I watched it land and went scarlet, because the wound kept bleeding long after. Cold hands, hot heart. I held the bandages. Reading: scarlet.",
      driftblade: "The greatsword is for our knight alone, so I kept out of the ground in front of him and swept round and through behind instead, and watched the wound keep running after, which seemed unfair, as a wound should stop when it is done.",
      marionettist: "The leading man's great dramatic stroke is for our knight alone; the rest of the cast keeps off the front of the stage. My Doll stays at his back. (The wound bled on through the next scene. Method acting.)",
      cleric: "The greatsword falls on our knight, and the wound stays open after. I lay the light on him before it lands, and keep mending until the bleeding stops. He winces and apologises. Hush, dear boy.",
      grovekeeper: "The greatsword falls on our knight like a felled oak, and the wound weeps after like sap. I plant the mending on him before the blow, and tend it while he bleeds. Slow work, for slow wounds.",
      chronomancer: "The greatsword lands on our knight, and the bleeding keeps time after it, drop by drop. I had him shielded before, and mended through the echo. I believe I have done this before. Several times, in the same order.",
      alembist: "The greatsword on our knight, then a bleeding that keeps on. Give the blue bottle before it falls and a slow green cure for the bleeding after, and keep both on the near shelf. Nearer. No, nearer. Ah.",
      threadweaver: "The greatsword landed on our knight, and I ran a thread to take some of it off him. The wound keeps pulling after; I held the seam mended till it closed, and did not let go.",
    } },
    'Barrow Stomp': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He stamped and the ground round his feet broke. I gave ground, out past the edge with the line, and came back to him after. A knight who retreats on purpose is not a retreating knight. That is what I told myself.",
      gloomsworn: "His stamp broke the floor about his feet. I gave it up, stepped out beyond the edge with the rest, and returned once it was still. The ground opens on its own schedule. I have no quarrel with it.",
      thresher: "A ring of earth shows at his feet before his foot comes down. I stepped back past its edge, let the ground heave, and walked straight back in. Broken ground after, but easily crossed.",
      bloodreaver: "He stamps, and the ground round him goes up. Tempting! But it's a dull hurt with nothing to it, so I stepped out with the others and came straight back to his face.",
      runeguard: "His foot raised, a ring of earth outlined about him: everyone, myself included, stands beyond its edge before it falls, then returns. The sign is generous with its warning. I appreciate that in a king.",
      gorgemaw: "A round platter of broken ground, served about the King's feet. I pushed back from the table with the others and came back after; some nights I take a bite. Crumbly, and gritty with centuries.",
      oathbroker: "Anyone within the ring at his feet is liable. Simple. His knights remember it, and present it again later. The undersigned stepped clear and returned for the rest.",
      ironpalm: "He stamped. A short word, said close. I stepped back out of its reach, then in again to answer. Not every word is worth standing under.",
      ranger: "Ring at its feet. I'm always outside it. The close ones had to step out. Easy to read, hard to forgive.",
      shadowblade: "The King stamped, and the floor all round him broke. Even his back is no shelter from that. I stepped out past the edge and slipped back in after. A guest of excellent manners always leaves before the crash.",
      pyromancer: "The floor round his feet glowed, and I was well out of it, for once, at a sensible distance! The ones close to him had to step out smartly. Entirely unsinged. Slightly smug. Mostly out of practice.",
      thermist: "Ring at his feet. Step out. Done. Then back in, hot, swinging before the dust had settled. The whole cycle in under a breath, thank you, and my fingers weren't even cold.",
      driftblade: "He stamps and the ground round him breaks, so I slipped out through the edge before it fell and came straight back in through the dust after, as if the dust were a door.",
      marionettist: "His Majesty stamps, and the floor about his feet breaks. The cast steps back to the wings; my Doll takes one dainty step out and returns on cue. (Light applause.)",
      cleric: "He stamps, and the ground about him breaks. Those close to him must step back out of it. Our brave knight stayed in once; it was the last time. I have never seen a man move so quickly.",
      grovekeeper: "The ground round him heaves like frost in early spring. My lot step back past the edge, then return. No planting needed, if nobody's standing in it.",
      chronomancer: "He lifts his foot; a moment later the ground about him breaks. Out on the lift, back in once it settles. A little dance. I have known the steps for ages.",
      alembist: "The ground at his feet, broken. Remedy: out of the ring before it falls, no reagents required. His knights serve it again later from where they stand; not on the label yet. Adding it now.",
      threadweaver: "He stamped, and everyone near him had to step back out of the ring. A neat little hem round him, and nobody inside it. Tack it down, and back in.",
    } },
    'Royal Doom': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He sentenced one of ours, never me. Lanterns appeared on the floor, and the doomed cannot lift one. I picked up the nearest and carried it to her side, boots loud on the flags. The light cured it. A good order is short and obeyed.",
      gloomsworn: "He named one of ours for doom. Lanterns burned about the hall, and the condemned cannot raise one. I lifted the nearest and carried it to her, and the light lifted the sentence. I have carried heavier to the dying.",
      thresher: "He laid his sentence on one of ours, and they cannot lift a lantern while it sits on them. So I fetched the nearest light over before it came due. Not their hour yet, I told the King. He did not laugh.",
      bloodreaver: "He dooms one of the others, never me, which is insulting. The doomed can't lift a lantern, so I grabbed one off the floor and ran it to them. Light cures it. Not a scratch to show for it. Dull rescue.",
      runeguard: "A sigil of death over one of us, never over me. Lanterns appear on the floor; another must lift one and bring its light near, for the doomed cannot. I am relieved to say I have the stamina for stairs and the wit for lanterns.",
      gorgemaw: "The King passes sentence on one of my guests. Lanterns appear about the hall; the doomed may not touch them, so I picked one up and brought it to the table. Light is the only sauce for it, and it is a thin sauce.",
      oathbroker: "Doom is a sentence on one of the undersigned, with a short term. The doomed may not take up a lantern; a co-signatory must carry one to them. I did. Settled. It is satisfying to cancel a death in writing.",
      ironpalm: "He named one of us for death. The doomed cannot lift a lantern. I carried one to her. The light answered him.",
      ranger: "Sigil over one of ours. Lanterns down round the hall; the marked one can't lift one. Nearest hand fetches it. Mine. Ran it over. Worked.",
      shadowblade: "The mark sentenced a friend, how tactless. The doomed cannot lift a lantern, so I slipped across the hall, took one off the floor and delivered it like a secret. Nobody saw. Nobody will ever thank me. Perfect.",
      pyromancer: "He doomed me! Me! I couldn't even lift a lantern, so I stood where everyone could reach me and waited, glowing with indignation, until someone brought the light. The humiliation of being rescued by a lamp.",
      thermist: "Marked for death. Chilled right through. The doomed can't lift a lantern, so I stood still where I could be found. Our knight brought one, and the cold went out of me at once. Result: warm. Very warm. Thank you.",
      driftblade: "He doomed our archer, who can't lift a lantern while doomed, so I skimmed through the hall to the nearest one, scooped it up on the wing, and through to her before it came due, and I did not even slow down.",
      marionettist: "His Majesty passes sentence on a member of the cast. The doomed may not touch the lanterns, so another must carry one on. (Enter the knight, stage right, with the light. Well done. Reprieved.)",
      cleric: "He passes sentence on one of us, and only the light answers it. The doomed cannot carry it themselves; someone else brings a lantern to them. I brought it this time, as fast as these legs allow.",
      grovekeeper: "His sentence lands on one of my lot like blight. Lanterns appear on the ground; the blighted can't carry one, so someone hale fetches it over. Light cures it the way sun cures frost.",
      chronomancer: "Sentence passed, on a short clock. The doomed cannot lift a lantern, so someone else must carry one over before the last tick. I did, with a moment to spare. I am, on balance, a punctual saviour.",
      alembist: "Doom is fatal if neglected. Cure: lantern light, carried over by someone not doomed, because the patient cannot dose herself. Spare lanterns gutter out once the cure is done. Do not store them near the paraffin. Learned that.",
      threadweaver: "He doomed one of mine, and she can't fetch a lantern herself. Another stitch carries the light to her; a lantern left on the floor helps nobody. Quick as a cut.",
    } },
    'Crown of Graves': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The whole hall broke open, all but the ground right at his feet. That is where I stand anyway. The line came in close behind me, where I could see them. It is a poor post for a coward and a fine one for a knight.",
      gloomsworn: "The graves opened across the hall, all but the stone at his own feet. That is my place anyway. I stood there, the others crowding in behind me, and the dark passed us by. It is the only floor in the Barrow that has never frightened me.",
      thresher: "The whole hall opened its graves except the patch right at his feet. I went in close against him, quiet as a shadow, and waited it out. The dead keep a polite distance from their lord.",
      bloodreaver: "The whole hall erupted except right up against the King, which is exactly where I live. The others came in and hugged him with me. Not even a graze, for once.",
      runeguard: "Graves open across the hall, but not at his feet: the quiet middle. Close in on him before it lands. I had drawn this on my wall already as a diagram; the reality was less tidy and equally effective.",
      gorgemaw: "The whole hall was served, except the little plate at the King's feet. I was already sitting there, and the others crowded in beside me. Too much table, not enough food. Cozy, though.",
      oathbroker: "The Crown of Graves claims every inch of the hall but the ground beside him. When a knight repeats it, the quiet is beside the knight. The undersigned closed in. The counterparty's own shadow is the only safe address.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke to the whole hall, but not to those beside him. I was beside him. The others came close. We listened in the quiet.",
      ranger: "Hate this one. Whole hall erupts but the ground at its feet. Gave up the distance and came right in close. Short line. Shorter temper.",
      shadowblade: "The whole hall broke open, all but the few paces round the mark. Close is where I live. The others had to come and crowd my lovely spot. Awkward company, but they paid for it in not dying.",
      pyromancer: "The whole hall lit up except the little ring at his feet! I had to give up my lovely distant spot and run right up to him. Hated every step. Survived beautifully. Eyebrows: whole.",
      thermist: "Everywhere but close. In, now. Then I sprinted, steaming, all the way to his feet, and the hall burst behind me like a pan of popping oil. Wonderful. Terrifying. I'd do it again, and my fingers are crossed.",
      driftblade: "The whole hall breaks open except right up against him, so I cut through the middle and in to his feet and circled there, close enough to touch, until it was done, which is the nearest I have come to standing still.",
      marionettist: "The old king claims the entire stage except his own spotlight. The cast rushes in to share it; my Doll too, at his very knee. (Crowded, but nobody missed the cue. A seat in the first row of death.)",
      cleric: "The whole hall breaks open except the quiet ground at his feet. I gathered everyone in close to him, near enough to touch, and nobody fell. A strange refuge, that.",
      grovekeeper: "The whole field heaves except the patch at his feet, like a frost that spares the hearth. My lot crowd in close to him, dear. Snug, if cold.",
      chronomancer: "On the downbeat the whole hall breaks open but the ground at his feet. In on the bar before, all of us. I ran in early; I always do. It is the one habit I can rely on.",
      alembist: "The whole hall cracked open, but not the ground at his feet, so stand there. From a knight the safe ground is at that knight's feet instead. Note for the shelf. Several shelves, actually. Everything rattles.",
      threadweaver: "Everywhere broke but the ground at his feet, so I pulled the whole party in tight round him. Not one loose stitch left out in the hall. It was cramped. It was alive.",
    } },
    'Echo Knights': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two dead knights rose by the walls. Each remembers one of his swings as he makes it, then swings it again from where it stands. I watch what each one learned and move the line to match. A soldier who learns from the enemy is a good soldier.",
      gloomsworn: "Two dead knights stood at the walls, each keeping a swing he had seen and sending it back from where he stood. I learned which swing each had kept and moved away from where it would go. They remember. So do I.",
      thresher: "His knights keep a swing apiece, the way a grave keeps its dead, and throw it up again from where they stand. I watched which swing flashed on which knight, and stood clear of that line when it came up.",
      bloodreaver: "His old guard rose and copied his swings, later, from where they stood. Two more friends to bleed for! I watch which swing each caught, and keep out of it anyway. Mostly.",
      runeguard: "A swing flashes on a knight as he makes it, and that knight now holds it. Read each knight's sigil, and when he replays it, treat it as the King's own, from there. Copyists! Even the dead cannot resist an annotation.",
      gorgemaw: "His guard reheat his dishes: each knight keeps one of his swings and serves it again from its own seat by the wall. Leftovers, but still hot. I note what each carried off, and dine accordingly.",
      oathbroker: "The knights are witnesses: each remembers one swing as it is made and testifies to it later, from where it stands, aimed at the middle. Their memory is exact. The undersigned avoided the testimony.",
      ironpalm: "His knights repeat his words. Each keeps one, and says it again from where it stands. I listen to what each one heard. Then I step aside.",
      ranger: "Two knights by the walls. Each keeps a swing it saw, replays it from there toward the middle. Watch which one it took. New angle, same shape.",
      shadowblade: "Two knights rise, and each memorises one of the mark's swings, then repeats it from its own spot by the wall. Copycats. I note which trick each has stolen, and stand where it cannot reach.",
      pyromancer: "Two knights rose and copied his swings! The nerve. Each repeats one, later, from its own spot. I watch which one each stole and move my lovely spot to match. I always did say imitation was the sincerest annoyance.",
      thermist: "Each knight takes one swing as he makes it. Keeps it cold. Replays it from its wall. Read the knight, not him. I moved, hot, and the floor burst behind me. Replication! Beautiful!",
      driftblade: "His knights each catch one of his swings and throw it again from the walls, so I watch which swing flashed on which knight and weave my path through whatever gaps they leave, which is, honestly, most of the room.",
      marionettist: "Two understudies by the walls, each learning one of the star's lines as he delivers it and performing it again later from their own marks. The cast watches who learned what. (Prompt corner, attentive.)",
      cleric: "Two of his old guard rose, each keeping one of his swings to use again from where they stand. I watch which one each knight took, and lead my friends clear of it. They were soldiers once. One forgets.",
      grovekeeper: "The knights keep his swings the way soil keeps seed, and each grows one again later from where it stands. My lot watch what each one took, and stand clear of the crop.",
      chronomancer: "The knights are his echo: each catches one swing as he makes it and plays it back later from the walls. I've always liked an echo. I dodged both. I may have dodged them yesterday.",
      alembist: "Two knights, each storing one of his swings and replaying it later from its own spot. Note which swing went to which knight, and dodge from that knight's position. Colour-code them. Red for left. I forget which is left.",
      threadweaver: "Two knights rose, each holding onto one of his swings to replay from the walls. I keep a thread on which one took what, and move everyone out of its way.",
    } },
    'The Charge Sheet': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Banners above his throne lit one at a time, each with one of his swings, and nothing fell. I said the order to the line until we had it, like drill. It comes back later. On the hard nights the order is drawn fresh; read the banners every time.",
      gloomsworn: "Banners over the throne glowed one after another, each carrying a swing of his. Nothing fell, so I said the order aloud until it stuck in all of us. It comes back. On the harder nights it is drawn anew; read the banners again.",
      thresher: "Banners lit over the throne in turn, a swing on each, and nothing came in. That is a promise, not a payment. I learned the order like steps in a dance, because it all comes back later; on the hard nights the order is new each time.",
      bloodreaver: "Banners light up over his throne, one swing on each, and nothing hits! Boring. But the King swings them all later, in that order, so I learned the order. Annoying. On the hard nights the order changes each pull, so I learned to read, not recite.",
      runeguard: "Banners over the throne, lit in turn, each bearing a swing's sigil, and nothing lands. Inscribe the order now, for Judgement returns it exactly. On the hard nights the order is drawn anew each pull. Inscribe it fresh. My favourite kind of task: a text with a deadline.",
      gorgemaw: "The King reads out the menu: banners above his throne, a course on each, in order. Nothing served yet. I memorised every dish; a good guest always knows what's coming. On the hard nights the menu changes each time; read it again.",
      oathbroker: "He reads the charges: the banners light in turn, each a swing, and nothing is collected yet. Judgement enforces the order exactly. On the hard nights the order is redrawn each hearing. Entered anew.",
      ironpalm: "He named his swings in order, on banners. Nothing more. I repeated them to myself until they were mine. Later, he says it all again. On the hard nights it changes each time. I listened again.",
      ranger: "Banners light over the throne. A sign on each, in turn. Remember them. They come back. Hard nights: order changes every pull. Read it fresh.",
      shadowblade: "The mark announced its plans on banners, in order, like an amateur. I committed every one to memory. Judgement is far less rude when you know the running order. On the hard nights the order is different every pull.",
      pyromancer: "Banners over his throne lit one after another, each with one of his swings! Nothing happened, so I nearly ignored it. Later I very much wished I hadn't. Remember the order! On the hard nights the order changes each time, so I watched properly.",
      thermist: "Banners, lit in turn. Learn the order cold, by heart, by rote, by song if necessary. When it comes back the swings come fast and hot, and there's no time to read them. Hard: order changes every pull. Learn it fresh.",
      driftblade: "The banners light up one after the other over his throne and nothing happens, which bores me, but I learned the order like a route, so later I knew which way to bank. On the hard nights the route changes each pull, so I watch the banners.",
      marionettist: "The star reads out his programme: banners above the throne, each act in order, nothing yet performed. The cast learns the running order now, or misses every cue later. On the hard nights the programme is rewritten. Read it again.",
      cleric: "The banners above his throne light one after another, each marked with one of his swings, and nothing falls. I whispered the order to my friends like a prayer, and we kept it. On the hard nights the order is drawn fresh, so we read it together.",
      grovekeeper: "The banners light in turn like the seasons, a swing on each. Nothing falls yet. Learn the order of the seasons, and you know what's coming. On the hard nights the seasons come in a different order each year. Read them again.",
      chronomancer: "He plays the melody once on the banners, a sigil for each note. Learn it now; later he plays it back fast. I knew it before he finished, which, admittedly, was last time. On the hard nights the melody changes. I listened properly.",
      alembist: "Banners over the throne, lit in order, one swing on each, and nothing lands yet. Write the order down somewhere you won't lose it. On the hard nights it is redrawn each pull. Re-write it. In ink. Not the green one.",
      threadweaver: "Banners lit above the throne one after another, a swing on each. Nothing fell. I basted the order into my memory, because it all comes back in sequence. On the hard nights it changes each pull, so I unpicked and re-sewed it every time.",
    } },
    'Coffin Lids': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He tore the lids off the coffins on both walls and sent them skidding across the floor. We stepped out of their path. Where they stop, they are cover for what comes next. I marked the nearest as my post and gave the order to take cover, since it was my turn to give one.",
      gloomsworn: "Lids flew off the coffins on both walls and slid across the hall. We stepped out of their paths. Where they stopped, they were shelter against the next blow. I rather liked them. A lid is a kind of grave that you can carry.",
      thresher: "Lids came skidding off the coffins from both walls, and I stepped out of their paths before they arrived. Where they settle is shelter, and the Decree comes right behind. A coffin lid is a friendly thing, if you know it.",
      bloodreaver: "Coffin lids skidding at me across the floor! I stepped out of their lanes, reluctantly. They stop out in the hall, and you'll want to hide behind them very soon. Hiding. Me. I know.",
      runeguard: "Lids rattle on either wall, their paths drawn across the floor. Clear the paths; once they stop, they stand as cover for the Decree that follows. I took note of the stopping places. A good scholar reads where a sentence ends.",
      gorgemaw: "He slid two heavy platters down the table from the walls. Not food, alas: coffin lids. Out of their way, then behind them, because the next course is the Decree.",
      oathbroker: "The lids come skidding from the walls along marked lines, and anyone in the path is liable. Where they come to rest is your shelter for the Decree. We read the rest-points before they arrived.",
      ironpalm: "He sent coffin lids along the floor. I stepped out of their path. They stopped. I stood behind one. Then he spoke again.",
      ranger: "Lids off the coffins, skidding in from both walls. Out of their lanes. Where they stop is cover. Mark it. Not complicated, once seen.",
      shadowblade: "Coffin lids came skidding across the hall from both walls. One steps out of their way, and then, naturally, behind one. Hiding is my whole vocation, and I took it up again with a clear conscience.",
      pyromancer: "Coffin lids came skidding at me from the wall! Out of the lane, then behind the lid. I have never been so glad to hide behind a coffin. Don't tell anyone. Especially not the knight.",
      thermist: "Lids from the walls, along straight lanes. Step aside. Then, where they stop, get behind one. Plain logic, for the wave that comes next. Wood, bone and fear. A passable shield.",
      driftblade: "Coffin lids came skidding along the floor from both walls and I sliced across their lanes before they arrived, and through, and doubled back behind one once it stopped, delighted to have something to hide behind for once.",
      marionettist: "Scenery arrives! Coffin lids slide on from both wings along their marks. The cast steps out of their way, then behind them for the next scene. (My Doll takes cover with great dignity.)",
      cleric: "The King tore the lids off his coffins and sent them skidding across the floor. Everyone out of their way, then behind one; those lids are shelter. Small mercies, and wood.",
      grovekeeper: "Coffin lids slide across the hall like logs on a flood, dear. My lot step out of their path, then shelter in their lee when they settle. The next storm is close behind.",
      chronomancer: "The lids skid in from the walls; a moment later, he decrees. Out of their path on the first beat, behind them on the second. I was hiding there already. It does feel like cheating.",
      alembist: "Coffin lids skidding in from the walls along fixed lanes: out of the lanes. They stop and stand as cover, and cover is needed straight after. Shelve the lids by the door. Ready, and quick.",
      threadweaver: "Lids came skidding in from both walls. We stepped out of their way, then tucked in behind them. Neat as a hem.",
    } },
    'Royal Decree': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A wave rolled down the hall from the throne. Only something solid between me and the throne kept it off, so I put a coffin lid there and kept my footing behind it. It cracked at the end, honest wood doing an honest job. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A wave poured down the hall from the throne. Open air will not stop it, only something solid, so I crouched behind a fallen lid with the throne on its far side. It was not the worst bed I have had.",
      thresher: "A wave rolls down the whole hall from the throne, and nothing standing in the open survives it. I crouched behind a fallen lid with the throne on its far side, as one waits out hail behind a wall.",
      bloodreaver: "A wave of grave-light down the whole hall. Can't take it head-on, not even me. Hid behind a coffin lid with the throne on its far side. Felt cowardly. Felt alive.",
      runeguard: "Light gathers at the throne; something solid between oneself and the throne, a lid or fallen stone, is the only answer, and the lid breaks after. I stood behind mine with my pen in hand. A fine place to write.",
      gorgemaw: "The Decree rolls down the hall from the throne, and there's no eating it. I crouched behind a coffin lid, throne on the far side. Some dishes are best left to pass. Cold. Gone off.",
      oathbroker: "The Decree binds all who are not sheltered. The shelter must stand between you and the throne, not merely nearby. A coffin lid sufficed, and broke. Material evidence of the court's severity.",
      ironpalm: "He decreed, and his voice rolled down the whole hall. I put a coffin lid between us and let it pass. The lid did not survive the sentence.",
      ranger: "Wave from the throne, down the whole hall. Needs something solid between you and it. Lid between me and the throne. Done.",
      shadowblade: "A wave of grave-light from the throne. One hides behind something solid, with the throne on its far side. I was born for this. Neither the King nor the wave found me.",
      pyromancer: "A great wave of grave-light came rolling down from the throne, so bright! I tucked in behind a coffin lid with the throne on its far side. Even my sleeves survived. A tribute to coffin craftsmanship.",
      thermist: "Wave from the throne. Need something solid between me and it. Lid. Crouched. Then the lid shattered and I came out hot, grinning, and so alive it felt like cheating.",
      driftblade: "A wave rolls down from the throne and there's no slipping through it, so I had to stop, which I hate, and crouch behind a coffin lid with the throne on its far side, counting the breaths until it was done.",
      marionettist: "The leading man's great decree sweeps the whole house from the throne. The cast hides behind the scenery, lids between them and him. (The scenery did not survive. The cast did. A fair exchange.)",
      cleric: "His decree rolled down from the throne like a cold tide. Only those with something solid between them and the throne were spared, so I gathered everyone behind the lids. Wood is wonderful.",
      grovekeeper: "The decree rolls from the throne like flood water. My lot sheltered behind the coffin lids, the throne on the far side, like lambs behind a wall.",
      chronomancer: "The decree comes just after the lids settle. Behind them, throne on the far side, by the downbeat. Next time, which was this time, I will have been there first.",
      alembist: "Light from the throne, down the whole hall: put something solid between you and the throne, lid or fallen stone. The lid breaks. A single-use container; I should have brought spares. Do I own spares? No.",
      threadweaver: "The decree rolled down from the throne, and only those with a lid between them and it were spared. I tucked the lot of them behind in a neat row, and put the needle in my hat.",
    } },
    'Funeral March': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "His dead marched across the hall in ranks, wall to wall. One lane through them stays open the whole way. I found it and brought the line into it, my footing never leaving the stone. The lane is somewhere new each time, and nothing paints it.",
      gloomsworn: "His dead walked the hall in ranks from wall to wall, and one lane stayed open the whole way. I found it and led the others through. It is never in the same place twice. They march well. I used to.",
      thresher: "His dead came across the hall in ranks, wall to wall, each rank tidy. There is always one lane left open right through, or the gap behind a rank once it passes; I found it and stood in it. It moves from pull to pull, and nothing paints it.",
      bloodreaver: "His dead came marching in ranks from wall to wall! Lovely, but not even I stand in front of that. There's always one open gap through them; I stood in it, sulking. The lane falls somewhere different each time, and nothing marks it.",
      runeguard: "Ranks of the dead form along one wall; one lane stays open from wall to wall, or the ground behind a rank once it has passed. On these nights it falls somewhere different each time, and is never painted. Read it off the ranks. I did.",
      gorgemaw: "His dead paraded across the table in ranks, a procession of bones. Far too dry to eat. There's always one gap in the procession; I sat in it. It opens somewhere different each time, unpainted, like a good seating plan.",
      oathbroker: "The March crosses the whole hall, rank upon rank. One lane stays open the whole way, and the ground behind a passing rank is safe. The lane is drawn anew each time, and nothing marks it. We found it by inspection.",
      ironpalm: "His dead marched across the hall. One silence ran through them, the same the whole way. I stood in it. It sits somewhere new each time. Listen for what is not there.",
      ranger: "Ranks of dead crossing the hall, wall to wall. One lane open all the way through. Found it, stood in it. Different every time, unmarked. Eyes first, feet second.",
      shadowblade: "The mark's dead marched through in ranks, wall to wall, and a gap is only a shadow with manners. I stood in the one they left. It was somewhere new each time, and nothing marked it.",
      pyromancer: "Rank after rank of his dead came marching through! I found the one gap they all left open and stood there, casting, while the parade went by. Mostly unsinged, if you don't count the rhythm. It was in a different place each time, unpainted.",
      thermist: "Ranks, wall to wall. Find the gap; it's the same for every rank. Stand in it. Then the last rank passed, and I ran hot after it. The lane moves each time and nothing shows where; read the ranks. Dizzy, joyful.",
      driftblade: "His dead marched through in ranks, wall to wall, and there's always one lane through them, or the gap behind a rank as it passes, so I went through behind one and kept going. It was somewhere new every time, so I read the gap.",
      marionettist: "A procession! Ranks of extras marching from one wing to the other. One aisle stays open all the way across, and the cast stands in it. (A spectacular scene.) The aisle is placed anew each night, and no one marks it.",
      cleric: "His dead marched across the hall, rank after rank. One lane through them stayed open, and I called everyone into it. All of us stood there in time. It falls somewhere different each time, unmarked.",
      grovekeeper: "His dead came across in rows like a harvest. One furrow stays open all the way through; my lot stood in it and let the rows pass by. It falls somewhere new each season, and nothing paints it.",
      chronomancer: "Rank after rank, evenly spaced, and one lane open on every beat. Stand in it and the march passes round you. I knew where it was. It lands somewhere new each time, unmarked, which is a flaw in the arrangement.",
      alembist: "Ranks of the dead across the whole hall: stand in the one lane they leave open, or on the floor just behind a rank once it passes. No brew. The lane is somewhere new each time, and never marked. Mark it with chalk? Not allowed.",
      threadweaver: "His dead marched across the hall in rows. One gap stays open all the way through, like the gap in a seam. We went into it, one by one. It opens somewhere new each time, unmarked.",
    } },
    'Barrow Bells': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two bells. Each wants a pair standing under it, or the whole line is doomed. I took a bell with a partner and held my ground beneath it, and did not look at the other bell once. I trusted the pair under it. That is duty too.",
      gloomsworn: "Two bells, and a pair must stand beneath each or we are all lost. I found a partner and stood under mine till the sound died. Being doomed is nothing new. I did not care to share it.",
      thresher: "Two bells, and each wants a pair of us under it, paired like pallbearers. Leave one bare and the whole hall goes over at once. We picked our partners well before it came due.",
      bloodreaver: "Two bells, two of us under each. Miss one and everyone gets doomed! I grabbed a partner and a bell and stood there. Not much fun, but nobody died. Tragic, in its way.",
      runeguard: "Two bell-rings are drawn on the floor; a pair must stand beneath each, decided before they are needed, for an unrung bell dooms us all. I chose my partner on the stairs. It is rude to leave a partner to chance.",
      gorgemaw: "Two bells, each laid for two diners. Leave a bell unrung and every guest is doomed. I sat at one with a friend. A shared plate, sadly. But no one went hungry for the right reason.",
      oathbroker: "The Bells require two signatories each; an unrung bell defaults on the whole party, every one of us doomed. The detail is the pair. I countersigned at one bell and watched the other like a hawk.",
      ironpalm: "Two bells, two voices to each. Leave one silent and all of us are doomed. I stood beneath one with a friend. We answered together.",
      ranger: "Two bells. A pair under each. Miss one, everyone's doomed. Took the far bell with the healer. Healer talks. Survived anyway.",
      shadowblade: "Two bells, each wanting a pair beneath it. I prefer to be alone, but an unrung bell dooms us all, so I stood under one with a friend. A friend. Out in the open. Unseen? Hardly, but it was worth it.",
      pyromancer: "Two bells, and each wants two of us under it! Miss one and everyone is doomed. I stood under mine with our knight, ringing beautifully. That is, the bell rang. I stood. Very well.",
      thermist: "Two bells, two people each. Leave one empty, everyone doomed. I bolted to mine and stood still beneath it with a friend, sweating cold. Even for me, that is an odd experience.",
      driftblade: "Two bells, two of us under each, and I had to stand still under mine with a friend, which I hate, because an empty bell dooms every one of us, so I stood, and counted the breaths, and survived the stillness.",
      marionettist: "Two bells, each requiring a duet beneath it. Leave one silent and the whole cast is doomed. Pairs, my dears, cast in advance. (My Doll does not count. She is a prop, and a very good one.)",
      cleric: "Two bells, each needing two of us beneath it; if one goes unrung, every one of us is doomed. I paired everyone off before the bells came, gently, like a matchmaker.",
      grovekeeper: "Two bells, two of my lot under each, like planting in pairs, dear. Leave one bare and the whole field is doomed. We paired up early.",
      chronomancer: "Two bells, two to each. Leave one empty and all of us are doomed. The pairs were set before the bells appeared. I chose mine already; I choose it again every time.",
      alembist: "Two bells, a pair under each, and an empty bell dooms everyone. Settle the pairs before the bells appear. Mine was our knight. A flask-sized fear, but shared. Safer with company.",
      threadweaver: "Two bells, two stitches threaded under each. Leave one empty and the whole hem is doomed. A bell without a pair is a dropped stitch, and I won't have it.",
    } },
    'Lights Out': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Every brazier went dark, then the pair nearest the King flared back up. Whoever stood outside their light was caught, so I got the line into the light. Standing in the open feels wrong. Standing in the dark feels worse.",
      gloomsworn: "The braziers died, then the two nearest the King lit again. Outside their glow you are found. I took us into that light and left the darkness to him. He is welcome to it. I have my own.",
      thresher: "Every brazier went out, then the pair nearest him took again. I got into that light before his eye came looking, the way one hurries in before the dark closes. Whoever is left out in the dark gets found.",
      bloodreaver: "Darkness! Then the braziers nearest the King flared back, and anyone outside their light got caught by his stare. I stood in the light, scowling. I like the dark. The dark doesn't love me back, here.",
      runeguard: "Every brazier dies; the pair nearest him flare back a breath later. Stand within their light before it lands, for his dread eye finds whoever stands in the dark. A text that is hard to read in the dark. I know. I tried.",
      gorgemaw: "The King blew out the candles. Then the pair nearest him relit, and anyone outside their glow was frozen. I sat by the fire. Atmosphere is everything at dinner, and so, as it turns out, is survival.",
      oathbroker: "All lights fail; the braziers nearest him return. One must be inside their light when it lands. Those left in the dark were held. A counterparty who prefers his debtors lit is a thorough one.",
      ironpalm: "The lights died. Two came back, near him. I stood in their light. In the dark, he speaks to you alone.",
      ranger: "All fires out. The two near it come back. Get in their light. The dark is where it catches you. Strange, to want a fire.",
      shadowblade: "Every light went out, and I was delighted, until it became clear this mark punishes the dark. The braziers nearest him relit, and I stood in the light like everyone else. Humiliating. I shall never speak of it.",
      pyromancer: "Every fire went out! An outrage! Then the braziers nearest him flared back, and I stood right in their light, glowing smugly. Out in the dark, you're caught. In the light, you're me.",
      thermist: "Fires out. Cold. The pair nearest him come back. Get to them, fast, hot. Stand in the light. Reading: warm. Alive. A bit singed. Excellent.",
      driftblade: "Every brazier died and then the two nearest him came back, so I went through the dark straight to the light and circled inside its edge until he'd looked and found nobody, and the glow was warm on my back.",
      marionettist: "Blackout! Then two spotlights, near His Majesty. The cast must stand in them before his eye falls on the dark. (Places, everyone. Into the light. The Doll, naturally, first.)",
      cleric: "Every fire in the hall went out, and then the two nearest him came back. I called my friends into their light. Those left in the dark were caught. A small light is a great mercy. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The braziers all went out like the end of a season, then the two nearest him rekindled. My lot crowded into their warmth. Those left in the dark were caught.",
      chronomancer: "Every fire out; a moment later two come back, near him. Into their light before the next beat. I was standing there when they lit. You learn where the fire will be.",
      alembist: "Total darkness: the braziers nearest him relight, so stand in their light, because in the dark he catches you. (My lamp went out too. Spilled oil. Not a metaphor.)",
      threadweaver: "The fires all went out, then the two nearest him came back. I drew everyone into their light before it landed. No loose threads left in the dark.",
    } },
    'Court of Bones': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He sat back on his throne, out of reach, and the coffins down the walls woke one by one. We broke each before it opened. When one did, I took the bones that climbed out, one at a time, and sent them back down.",
      gloomsworn: "He withdrew to his throne, out of reach, and the coffins along the walls woke one by one. We cracked each before it opened. Whatever got out of one, I met. They did not seem surprised either.",
      thresher: "He settled on his throne and the coffins along the walls began to glow one after another. I cracked each before it could open, in the same order every time. Bones belong below. Room for one more? Not tonight.",
      bloodreaver: "The King sat down, and coffins woke along the walls. Break them before they open! One opened and a skeleton crawled out. I let it bite me first, then broke it. Hardly a bite. Needs more teeth.",
      runeguard: "A coffin glows as it nears opening, in a fixed order down the walls. Break each before it opens; the order never changes. I wrote it on my sleeve. Do not, I add, judge a scholar by his sleeves.",
      gorgemaw: "The King retired from the table and left coffins to serve. Each glows before it opens; break it first, or a skeleton climbs out. All bone, no meat. I caught one anyway. Thin soup.",
      oathbroker: "A recess: he retired to his throne while the coffins woke, each in turn. Break each before it opens, or it releases a skeleton. The undersigned settled most of them. A few were contested.",
      ironpalm: "He sat. The coffins spoke, one by one. We broke each before it opened. One opened. I answered it.",
      ranger: "It sat down, out of reach. Coffins along the walls glow, then open. Break each first. Shot them in order. Easiest targets of my life.",
      shadowblade: "The mark retired to his throne, and the coffins along the walls woke one by one. Break each before it opens. Coffins have no eyes; my favourite kind of mark. I crept along the wall like a child with a candle.",
      pyromancer: "He sat down to watch while coffins woke along the walls! Break each one before it opens. I set them alight in order, one at a time. Delightful. A little crowd-pleasing, even. They were a dead audience.",
      thermist: "Coffins, one by one, glowing. Order! Break each before it opens. A skeleton crawled out of one I missed, and I swore, steaming. Missed one. Annoyed. Thrilled. Missed one, in a row of twenty.",
      driftblade: "He sat on his throne and the coffins woke down the walls one after another, so I swept along the bays breaking each one before it could open, the best part of the whole fight, honestly, a proper run.",
      marionettist: "Intermission! His Majesty retires to his throne while coffins awaken down the walls. The cast breaks each before it opens. (My Doll smashed several. Bravo.)",
      cleric: "He sat on his throne and woke the coffins down the walls, one by one. My friends broke them before they opened. One skeleton got out, and I kept our knight on his feet. Gently, now, gently.",
      grovekeeper: "Coffins woke along the walls like seeds in spring, one at a time. My lot broke each before it could sprout. Weeding, really.",
      chronomancer: "The coffins wake one at a time, evenly spaced, in the same order. Break each before its turn. I knew the order. I did. It was a pleasure.",
      alembist: "Coffins waking one by one: break each before it opens, because an opened coffin spoils into a skeleton. Clear the shelf in order. Left to right, labelled, no dust. (There was dust.)",
      threadweaver: "He sat on his throne while the coffins woke, one by one. We broke them before they opened. Tidy work. One seam at a time, snip, snip.",
    } },
    'Sceptre Throw': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "From his throne he hurled his sceptre at whoever stood farthest from him, and the aim followed them to the last moment. I made sure the farthest was someone who could walk clear. On the harder nights the two farthest each take one.",
      gloomsworn: "A sceptre from the throne, aimed at whoever stood farthest and following them to the last. I chose the farthest; it had to be someone quick. The harder nights send one at each of the two.",
      thresher: "The sceptre flew from the throne at whoever stood farthest out, the aim trailing them like a hound. I was the far one once; I led it down the hall and stepped off at the last. On the hard nights it wants the two farthest.",
      bloodreaver: "The King threw his sceptre from the throne at whoever stood farthest from him! The line tracks you till a breath before. Walk off the line, don't stand there admiring it. Harder nights, the two farthest.",
      runeguard: "A line from the throne finds the farthest of us and follows them. Whoever stands farthest leads it away and steps off before it lands; on harder nights, the two farthest. I made myself farthest, once, for the experiment.",
      gorgemaw: "The King threw his sceptre across the room at the guest sitting farthest away. It follows them till the last moment. Keep moving, step off. On harder nights he throws at the two farthest.",
      oathbroker: "The Sceptre Throw is directed at the undersigned farthest from the throne, and tracks him until a moment before it lands. Terms: step aside in time. On the harder terms, the two farthest are named.",
      ironpalm: "He threw his sceptre from the throne at the farthest of us. The line followed. I stepped off it. A thrown word is only heard if it lands.",
      ranger: "Throne to farthest target. Line tracks till just before. Stepped off. Hard: two farthest. Standing far has a price.",
      shadowblade: "The mark hurled his sceptre from the throne at whoever stood farthest from him. Not me, naturally; I stay close. The line follows until the last moment. Step off it. Another reason to love the near side.",
      pyromancer: "He flung his sceptre from the throne at whoever was farthest away! That line follows you around! I stepped off right at the end, like a very graceful cat. Hard nights, the two farthest. Be graceful twice.",
      thermist: "Sceptre, throne to the farthest of us, tracking until the last moment. Step off. I was the farthest, and I ran sideways, shrieking. Two farthest on hard. Statistical misfortune.",
      driftblade: "He threw his sceptre from the throne at the farthest of us and the aim chased me, so I cut across it, not along it, and it landed behind me, and I felt like a hawk dodging a hunter. On harder nights it goes for the two farthest.",
      marionettist: "The king hurls his sceptre from the throne at the player farthest from him. The line follows the performer until the last beat; step off the mark. (Two players on a hard night. Double the drama.)",
      cleric: "He hurled his sceptre from the throne at whoever was farthest from him. The line followed them till just before it landed; I called them to step sideways. Two of us on harder nights. I have never been so grateful to be second-farthest.",
      grovekeeper: "A sceptre flew from the throne at the farthest of my lot, the line following them like a vine till the last moment, dear. They stepped off. On harder nights it flies at the two farthest.",
      chronomancer: "The sceptre flies from the throne to whoever is farthest, the line tracking until the last breath. Step off. I was somewhere else. On harder nights, the two farthest, which is a different somewhere.",
      alembist: "A sceptre thrown from the throne at the farthest of us, tracking until a moment before: step off the line in time. Two farthest on harder nights. Whoever is farthest, beware. Stay near the cupboard.",
      threadweaver: "He threw his sceptre from the throne at my farthest stitch, the aim following her until the end. She stepped off it. On harder nights it goes for the two farthest. Nobody wanders when I am watching.",
    } },
    'Grave Summons': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The dead gathered on one of the young ones, never me, and the rest of us had to close on them, all but one. On harder nights a mark settles over each of us the moment it lands. Close in, then out. Stranger than drill.",
      gloomsworn: "The dead came together over one of the young ones, never me, and all but one of us went in to share it. On the harder nights marks fall on each of us right after: in together, then apart. I was never good at crowds. I learned.",
      thresher: "The dead came up round one of the young ones, never our knight, and we crowded in on her, every hand but one. On the hard nights a ring comes over each head straight after: in for the one, then out to your own ground.",
      bloodreaver: "The dead gathered on one of the others! Everyone pile in, nobody left out. And on the harder nights, straight after, a mark over each head. In, then out. I like it when the room runs.",
      runeguard: "The dead gather on one of the party, never the knight. All but one gather on them; on harder nights, the marks follow close behind, so scatter. I annotated this with arrows. In, then out. Two arrows, opposite directions.",
      gorgemaw: "The dead sat down at the table beside one guest, and the rest of us were asked to join. All but one, please. On hard nights, small plates follow a few breaths after: scatter. Crowded, then lonely.",
      oathbroker: "The dead assemble upon one of the undersigned, never the shield. Terms: all but one attend. On the harder terms, a separate mark follows each of us moments later. Attend, then disperse.",
      ironpalm: "The dead gathered on one of us. We went to them. On the harder nights, marks came after. We scattered. Together, then apart.",
      ranger: "Dead gathered on one of the others, not the knight. All but one in. Hard nights: marks follow close behind. In, then out. Short commute.",
      shadowblade: "The dead gathered on someone else, which is rude of them. I went in, then out. On the harder nights, marks over each of us come a few breaths later. Keep moving.",
      pyromancer: "The dead gathered on one of us and everyone had to pile in! Then, on hard nights, a mark on every head moments later: scatter! Cosy, then cruel. Hat still on.",
      thermist: "Dead gather on one of the party; all but one in. Hard: marks over every head a few breaths on. In, out. Pack, then flee! Gather, then scatter! Like an experiment with a very short half-life.",
      driftblade: "The dead gathered on one of us and I had to come in and stand close, which I hate. Then on hard nights a mark over each head moments later and I was straight out again, relieved to be moving.",
      marionettist: "The dead gather on one player, never the lead. The cast closes on them, all but one; on a hard night, the marks follow close behind. (Close ranks, then fan out. A pretty piece of choreography.)",
      cleric: "The dead gathered on one of my friends, never our knight. We closed on them, all but one, and on harder nights the marks came moments later, so we scattered at once. I mended everyone after.",
      grovekeeper: "The dead gathered on one of my lot like moss on a stone. We drew in close. On harder nights the marks follow soon after, and the moss must be scattered.",
      chronomancer: "The dead gather on one of us, never the knight. Everyone in, all but one. On harder nights the marks follow moments later: in, then out. I was already moving. I had moved before.",
      alembist: "The dead gather on one of us, so all but one close on them; on harder nights, a mark on each of us moments later, so out again at once. Crowd, then disperse. Do not run with flasks.",
      threadweaver: "The dead gathered on one of my lot. I drew the rest in, all but one; on harder nights the marks follow moments later, and the threads must go slack again at once.",
    } },
    'Grave Marks': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A mark over every one of us, each wanting its own ground. The line opened up, each man to his own patch, then closed again. Spacing is the oldest drill there is. It was good to see it done well.",
      gloomsworn: "Marks settled over every one of us. Each needed room to themselves, so we opened out, took our own places, and came together again when it was over. Alone together. I know the feeling.",
      thresher: "A ring came up over every head, and each of us wanted a corner of our own. I took one to myself, no ring touching another, and walked back in once it had come in.",
      bloodreaver: "A mark over every head, mine too. Mine would hurt the others, so I took my own patch of floor. Two marks on one person is the one that hurts. Never been so polite in my life.",
      runeguard: "A circle at everyone's feet. Each of us on our own ground, no circle touching another. I paced mine out, as a surveyor would, and then re-paced it, as a scholar must.",
      gorgemaw: "A small plate under everyone. Each dines alone tonight. I kept to my own table; some nights I eat a friend's portion as well. Not this one, however hungry.",
      oathbroker: "Each of the undersigned is marked and individually liable. No circle may overlap another's. Separate accounts, separate arrears.",
      ironpalm: "A mark over every head. Each spoke alone. I gave each its own floor. Even a crowd can hold its silence.",
      ranger: "Marks on every head. Fanned out like a hunting party, each to our own ground. Easy. Wonderful, almost. Quiet.",
      shadowblade: "A mark over everyone's head, mine included, which was unforgivably public. I slid off to my own patch, alone, as I prefer.",
      pyromancer: "A mark over every head! Each of us needed our own floor, so I claimed a grand patch all to myself. A stage of my own at last. Nobody singed.",
      thermist: "Marks over every head. Apart: each to our own floor. Then back in, hot. Every experiment needs a control; I am happy to be mine.",
      driftblade: "Marks over all our heads and each needed its own floor, so I swept out through the gaps to the far side and circled there alone and came back through after, grateful for the room to turn.",
      marionettist: "Every member of the cast marked, each needing a solo spot. The troupe fans out across the stage. (A rare scene with everyone in the spotlight. A feast for the front row.)",
      cleric: "A mark over everyone's head, each needing their own floor. I sent each of my friends to their own patch, and mended them all after. Even I take a patch to myself, when the light requires it.",
      grovekeeper: "A mark over every head, like seedlings needing their own soil. My lot planted themselves apart, and I watered them all after.",
      chronomancer: "Marks over every head; on the next beat each lands. Each of us apart by then. I was alone already, and I will be, again, in a moment.",
      alembist: "A mark on everyone: spread out, no circles touching, and a dose for all after. One small fire, deliberate. The rest, accident. Tidy now.",
      threadweaver: "A mark over each head. I let the threads go slack so each stitch had its own floor, then pulled them all tight again after. A sound seam, loosened on purpose.",
    } },
    'The King Rises': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He rose from his throne, and his knights took new posts along the walls. The line had to learn new ground. I took him back and turned him away from them. A change of posting. The sergeant hated those.",
      gloomsworn: "He left the throne and his knights took new posts on the walls. All I had learnt of the room was wrong. I drew him back and turned his face from the others. A new watch, new corners. Same dark.",
      thresher: "He got up off his throne, and his knights moved to fresh ground along the walls. Every echo comes from their new places now, so I walked the hall again before trusting it. Moved earth looks different.",
      bloodreaver: "The King stood up again! And his knights moved, so everything from the walls came from new places. Back to his face, at last. I missed it. Sentimental, me.",
      runeguard: "He rises, and his knights move to new posts. Read the hall again; every echo now comes from where they stand now, not where they stood. I tore a page out and drew a new one.",
      gorgemaw: "The King returned to the table, and his guard changed seats. The leftovers come from new places now. I took my seat across from him again, and the napkin was cold.",
      oathbroker: "He resumed, his knights in new positions. Their testimony now comes from the new ones. The undersigned noted the change and amended the schedule accordingly.",
      ironpalm: "He stood. His knights moved. Their words come from new places now. I went back to him, and listened again.",
      ranger: "It rose. Knights moved to new walls. New angles. Read them again. Good practice.",
      shadowblade: "The mark rose, and his knights took new places by the walls. His back is still where it always was. I went straight back there, like a cat to a warm spot.",
      pyromancer: "He rose from his throne and his knights moved to new places, so everything they copy comes from somewhere new! I moved my spot, grumbling. A fresh stage. The same audience.",
      thermist: "He rose, and the knights shifted. New walls, new angles, learned in a breath. Then hot, and back in. Hypothesis reset. Stand by.",
      driftblade: "He rose and the knights shifted to new walls, so the lanes I'd worked out were wrong, and I flew a fresh circuit through the hall to learn the new ones, delighted, like a hawk on a new hill.",
      marionettist: "The star returns from intermission! The understudies take new marks on the walls, and the cast re-blocks everything. (Entr'acte concluded. Everyone to their new places.)",
      cleric: "He rose from his throne and his old knights took new places. I gathered my friends and showed them where the echoes would come from now. A little talk before the second act, nothing more.",
      grovekeeper: "He rose like a frost lifting, dear, and his knights moved to new ground. Their weather comes from new fields now. My lot adjusted.",
      chronomancer: "He rises; the knights move. The echoes come from new places now. I adjusted before it happened. That is, I think I did. Next time I will have.",
      alembist: "He rises, and his knights move to new posts. Relabel the shelves: the echoes come from new places now. Left shelf is now the right. Don't ask. Everything has swapped.",
      threadweaver: "He rose, and his knights moved to new walls. I pinned everyone to the new pattern. Unpick the old; sew the new.",
    } },
    'Royal Tithe': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He took his tithe, and every one of us dropped low at once. Shields laid beforehand stayed. He kept his blows off me while the healers mended the line before the Dirge. We held, and I will not say how close.",
      gloomsworn: "Everyone dropped low at the tithe, together. Shields laid beforehand survived it. I held the King's attention while the healers pulled us up ahead of the Dirge. A man may be borrowed from, and live.",
      thresher: "He took his tithe of every one of us at once, the way a collector takes the best of a house. Shields laid on before it stay; it is only life he wants. I kept near the menders till they had me whole before the Dirge.",
      bloodreaver: "The King took his tithe: every one of us dropped near death at once. Glorious! For me. The others needed shields before it and mending after, before the Dirge. I was just happy to be invited.",
      runeguard: "He raises a hand over all of us. Shields laid before, for they are not life and he cannot take them; then every one of us mended before the Dirge. I laid mine on the pedantic grounds that I would rather be right than rich.",
      gorgemaw: "The King demanded a tithe: everyone drained low at once. Shields aren't life; he can't take them. The Dirge follows quickly, so mend before it. A meal that leaves you hungrier than before.",
      oathbroker: "The Tithe levies life from every one of the undersigned at once. Shields are not life and stay; the Dirge comes soon after. Mend everyone before it falls due. A cunning court takes everything but what is paid in advance.",
      ironpalm: "He took his tithe of all of us. The shields stayed. Then quiet. Mend, before the Dirge. I let the breath return, and then the breath was enough.",
      ranger: "Tithe drops all of us low. Shields stay. Then the Dirge. Healers mend first. Kept drawing. Not a fun ten breaths.",
      shadowblade: "The mark took a tithe of life from all of us at once. Very rude. Shields stay, and the healers mended everyone before the Dirge. He never saw me tremble, which is more than I can say for my knees.",
      pyromancer: "He took a tithe! Every one of us dropped low! I felt terribly pale. Our shields stayed, the healers mended us, and the Dirge washed over us harmlessly. A bit of colour on my cheek, at last.",
      thermist: "Tithe. Everyone low. Cold, cold. Shields stay. Then mending, fast, hot, before the Dirge. I was shaking so much I recorded nothing. Reading: lamentable. Alive.",
      driftblade: "He took his tithe and every one of us dropped low at once, so I stayed close to the healers for once, still circling, and let them mend me before the Dirge, and felt my breath come back.",
      marionettist: "The star takes his tithe from the whole cast, and everyone drops low. Shields remain; the menders bring everyone back up before the Dirge. (A tense scene. Costumes torn.)",
      cleric: "He took his tithe from all of us at once. Shields aren't life, so I laid them first. Then I mended everyone before the Dirge. Even in the dark there is a little light, and it is a clerical duty to carry it.",
      grovekeeper: "The tithe takes from all my lot at once, like a drought. Plant shields before; they stay. Then mend everyone before the Dirge.",
      chronomancer: "The tithe lands; a moment later, the Dirge. Shields before the first note, mending between. I did it before and I did it again, and the second time was better. It usually is.",
      alembist: "The Tithe drains all of us low. Shields before, since he can't take those, then a big dose for everyone before the Dirge. Shelves emptied. All the good bottles gone. I regret nothing.",
      threadweaver: "He took his tithe from everyone at once. Shields before; they stay. Then I pulled the whole hem back up before the Dirge. It hung by a stitch. I held the stitch.",
    } },
    'Dirge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The Dirge tolled through all of us after the tithe. No blocking it. The line came to it whole, mended in time. It is no honour to be spared by a song, but I took the honour.",
      gloomsworn: "The Dirge rang for everyone right behind the tithe, with nothing to stop it. The healers had us whole. That is the whole of the answer. He sings badly.",
      thresher: "The Dirge follows the tithe close, and anyone still low goes over with it. I made sure I was mended before it came in. Not much time, but enough, if the menders start early.",
      bloodreaver: "The Dirge comes right after the tithe. Small, but if you're still low it kills you. I was mended. Not a mark on me. A thin song for a thick-skinned man.",
      runeguard: "The Dirge follows the tithe a breath later. Everyone mended before it tolls. I made a note in the margin: beautiful, mournful, entirely survivable. The best songs are.",
      gorgemaw: "The Dirge is the after-dinner song, sung to all, and it kills those left hungry from the tithe. Mended before. Dull tune.",
      oathbroker: "The Dirge is the final instalment of the Tithe, due from all. One must be mended before it. A lender who sings is still a lender.",
      ironpalm: "The Dirge spoke to all of us. Those still low could not have answered. We were mended.",
      ranger: "Dirge after the tithe. Everyone, no cover. Be mended before. I was.",
      shadowblade: "The Dirge, sung softly. It takes only those still low from the tithe, and I was mended. A song for the unseen, almost.",
      pyromancer: "The Dirge tolled through all of us! Only a soft song, but anyone still low from the tithe would have dropped. I was mended in time, and a little moved.",
      thermist: "Dirge after the tithe. Soft, cold. Kills only the low. Mended in time. Mildly insulted that it was so quiet.",
      driftblade: "The Dirge tolls right after the tithe, soft, but fatal to anyone still low, so I got myself mended before it, still circling, humming a better song.",
      marionettist: "The Dirge, sung to the whole cast. Fatal only to those still low from the tithe. The menders brought everyone up in time. (A sombre number.)",
      cleric: "Right behind the tithe came the Dirge, so soft it is easy to forget it kills. I had everyone mended first, and hummed along, a better tune.",
      grovekeeper: "The Dirge follows the tithe like rain after drought. Soft, but my lot must be green again before it comes. They were.",
      chronomancer: "The Dirge, just after the tithe. Soft, but it finishes the low. I mended them all in time. I have the tune by heart: it ends the same way.",
      alembist: "The Dirge: soft, but fatal to the low. Everyone mended before it, same shelf as the Tithe. Mind the stoppers. Low volume, high stakes.",
      threadweaver: "The Dirge tolled through the whole hem after the tithe. Soft, but it snaps any thread still frayed. I had them all mended.",
    } },
    'Judgement': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Judgement: the swings from the banners, in that order, from him and both knights at once, each shown for an instant. I called the order to the line from memory, loud as drill. Not one man was in the wrong place.",
      gloomsworn: "Judgement is the banners made flesh: all their swings in the old order, thrown by him and both knights together, each glimpsed for a heartbeat. I spoke the order as it came. A condemned man remembers his sentence.",
      thresher: "Judgement hands back every banner swing in the order they were shown, from him and both knights at once. I had learnt the order earlier, so I was moving before each landed, and ended close in for the Crown. As rehearsed.",
      bloodreaver: "Judgement! All the banner swings, from him and both knights at once, in order, barely shown. I knew the order and stepped right. Not a scratch. Half sorry.",
      runeguard: "The charges returned in order, from him and his knights together, each barely drawn. The order inscribed earlier; move a beat before each shows, and close on him for the Crown. I have never been so glad of my handwriting.",
      gorgemaw: "Judgement: the menu returns, every course at once, from the King and both his knights, each barely glimpsed. I knew the order. Ate nothing; dodged everything. A rare triumph of memory over appetite.",
      oathbroker: "Judgement enforces the charges in their listed order, from him and his knights together, each shown only briefly. The relevant schedule was in the Charge Sheet. The undersigned recalled it. Closing arguments went well.",
      ironpalm: "Judgement. Everything he said on the banners, said again, all at once, from all of them. I knew the words before they came. I answered.",
      ranger: "Judgement. Banner order, from it and both knights at once, barely shown. Moved before each one landed. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "Judgement: every swing on the banners, in order, from the mark and both knights at once, each shown only an instant. I knew the order. Nobody saw me move.",
      pyromancer: "Judgement! Every swing from the banners, from him and both knights, in order, barely shown! I remembered! I moved! Rather good, I think.",
      thermist: "Judgement. The banners' order, cold in memory. Then the swings, hot and fast, from him and both knights. Move ahead of each. I danced it. It wasn't elegant. It was correct.",
      driftblade: "Judgement throws the banner swings back in order from him and both knights at once, barely shown, so I flew the route I'd learned from the banners, through each gap a step early, and it felt like flying.",
      marionettist: "Judgement: the programme performed in full, in order, by the star and both understudies at once, every scene barely lit. The cast knew the running order. (Standing ovation.)",
      cleric: "Judgement: every swing from the banners, in order, from him and both knights at once. I led my friends through it from memory, close to him for the ring. Not one of them looked back.",
      grovekeeper: "Judgement: all the banner seasons at once, in order, from him and both knights. My lot knew the order and moved before each, dear, like geese before frost.",
      chronomancer: "Judgement plays the banner melody back fast, from him and both knights. I knew the tune; we moved on the beat before each note. I might have hummed it. I may have been humming it all week.",
      alembist: "Judgement: every banner swing in order, from him and both knights at once. The order, memorised earlier: out of each circle, out of each front, in close for the ring. Memory, it turns out, stores better than any shelf.",
      threadweaver: "Judgement: every banner swing in order, from him and both knights. We moved together, from memory, like a well-drilled needle-line. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'The Long Night': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The braziers died and one lantern was left. Doom passed from one of us to the next, the next one's sigil glowing ahead, and whoever was cured carried the light on. A relay, in the dark. I have never run so carefully.",
      gloomsworn: "Every light failed but one lantern. Doom moved along our line, each next one glowing before their turn, and the lantern went with the cure. I kept the watch while it passed from hand to hand. It is a thing I know how to do.",
      thresher: "One lantern left, and the doom passes down our line like a cold draught. The next one glows ahead of time, so whoever holds the light walks it over, and they carry it on in turn. Nobody went over before their hour.",
      bloodreaver: "The Long Night: one lantern left, and doom passing round us. The next one glows ahead; the lantern goes to them, and they carry it on. I carried it once. Grim and wonderful.",
      runeguard: "The next to be doomed glows ahead of time. Whoever holds the lantern walks it to them; once cured, they carry it on to the next. A sequence, rendered in light. I kept the list.",
      gorgemaw: "The King blew out every candle but one. Doom passes round the table, and the next guest glows ahead. Bring the lantern to them; they carry it on. A dessert to share, and an odd one.",
      oathbroker: "The Long Night: a single lantern, and Doom passed from one co-signatory to the next. The next glows ahead. Bring the lantern; they inherit it. A chain of custody, in the dark.",
      ironpalm: "The dark came. One lantern. Doom passed among us, the next one glowing ahead. I brought the light. Then they carried it. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Last lantern. Doom moves round us. Next one glows ahead. Carry the light to them; they carry it on. Longest walk of my life, shortest actual distance.",
      shadowblade: "One lantern left in the dark, and doom going round us. The next to be doomed glows ahead, and I carried the light across to them. For once, I wanted to be seen.",
      pyromancer: "Every fire out but one lantern! Doom hops from one of us to the next, with a glow to warn you, and I carried the light across. For once I was not the brightest thing in the hall.",
      thermist: "One lantern. Doom moves. Next one glows. Carry it to them. Then they take it on, and I fidget in the warmth. Relay, relay! Lovely baton.",
      driftblade: "The Long Night: one lantern, doom passing from one of us to the next, so I carried it through the dark to whoever glowed next, and they took it on from there, and for once I did not mind being slow.",
      marionettist: "The finale: every light out but one lantern, passed hand to hand as doom moves through the cast. The next one glows ahead. (A beautiful relay. Quite the closing image.)",
      cleric: "The Long Night: one lantern, and doom passing among us. The next one glows ahead; whoever holds the light brings it to them. I thought a good deal about that as I walked. Thank the light, quite literally.",
      grovekeeper: "One lantern, and doom moving through my lot like a long winter. The glow ahead tells who is next; the light goes to them, and they carry it on.",
      chronomancer: "The Long Night: doom passes from one to the next, the next glowing a moment ahead. The lantern follows. I carried it already. I carry it again. The night is long, but the beats are short.",
      alembist: "The Long Night: one lantern, doom passing round. Carry the lantern to whoever glows next; once cured, they carry it on. One reagent, shared. The cleverest recipe in the book: use one flask, and pass it on.",
      threadweaver: "One lantern, and doom passing from one to the next. The next glows ahead; the light goes to them, and they carry it on. A single thread, handed down the row.",
    } },
    'Gravebind': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A grave-chain bound two of us, never me. They kept close until it broke while the rest spread under their marks. I took the greatsword. Three duties, and the pair is drawn fresh every pull.",
      gloomsworn: "A chain of the grave tied two of us together, never me. They stayed close till it broke while everyone else kept clear of each other under their marks, and I had the great sword to deal with. Three jobs at once, and the pair is new every time.",
      thresher: "A chain grew between two of us, never the knight, and a fresh pair every time. The two on the chain kept close as a pair of dancers until it snapped; the rest of us spread out under our rings.",
      bloodreaver: "A grave-chain on two of the others, a different pair each pull! They huddled; the rest scattered under their marks. I took the greatsword. Busy night, three things at once. I loved it.",
      runeguard: "A chain between two of the party, never the knight, a different pair each time. The bound pair stay close until it breaks; the others apart under their marks; and I take the greatsword. Three scripts, one stage.",
      gorgemaw: "A chain bound two of my guests together, and it was a different two than last time. They stayed close; the others dined apart. I had the greatsword. Several courses at once, and none of them cooked.",
      oathbroker: "Gravebind joins two of the undersigned, never the shield, a different pair each hearing. They must stay close until it breaks; the others must keep apart. I took the greatsword. Concurrent liabilities, three of them.",
      ironpalm: "A chain bound two of us. A different two each time. They stayed close. The others kept apart. I took the greatsword. A busy silence.",
      ranger: "Chain on two of us, never the knight. The pair stuck close till it snapped; the others kept their distance, marks overhead. Different pair each time, which is annoying.",
      shadowblade: "A grave-chain bound me to another, a different one each night. We kept close until it broke; the rest kept apart. Very public. It never saw me, at least.",
      pyromancer: "A chain tied me to someone else, so we stood nose to nose until it snapped. Different pair each time. Everyone else kept apart under their marks. Terribly intimate. I did not know what to do with my hands.",
      thermist: "Chained to another, not the same one as last time. Stay close. Others apart, under marks. Then it broke, and we stepped apart, steaming. Two bodies, one temperature. Briefly.",
      driftblade: "A grave-chain bound me to somebody, so I couldn't roam, so I circled them close until it broke, while the others kept apart under their marks. Never the same pair twice, and I finally had company on the wing.",
      marionettist: "A grave-chain binds two of the cast together, never the lead, and they stay close until it breaks. The pairing changes every night. The rest keep apart. (My Doll and I were not bound. A relief.)",
      cleric: "A grave-chain bound me to one of the others, a different pair every night. We kept close until it broke. A little company in the dark is no hardship.",
      grovekeeper: "A chain bound two of my lot together, like vines grown into each other. A different two each season. We stayed close until it broke. The rest kept apart.",
      chronomancer: "A chain bound two of us, never the knight, a different two each time. We stayed close until it broke, a few breaths later. The others apart. I knew who. I do not know how.",
      alembist: "Gravebind: two of us, chained, drawn fresh each pull. Stay close until it breaks, the rest apart under marks, and the greatsword falls on our knight at the same time. Three bottles uncorked, three hands on them.",
      threadweaver: "A grave-chain bound two of my stitches, a different two every time. They kept close, no slack, until it broke. The rest kept apart. Slack in the wrong place, and the whole hem comes apart.",
    } },
    'Burial Rites': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He dug a grave for each of us, each in our own colour. Which is whose changes every pull, so read your colour. Lines out, in close for the crown, then into my own grave before it shut. I have slept in worse beds.",
      gloomsworn: "He dug a grave apiece for us in the hall's centre, each its own colour, and shuffled whose is whose every time. I checked the colour of mine. Out wide for the lines, tight for the crown, then down into my grave before it shut. I have lain in one already.",
      thresher: "He dug a grave for each of us in our own colours, in fresh places every time. In close for his crown, then each out to their own to lie still. Never another's; an empty grave brings the dead up for everyone. I do like a tidy grave.",
      bloodreaver: "The King dug us graves! One each, our own colour, but the places shuffle every pull. In close for his crown, then out and lie in your own. The one who strayed got a lovely wound, and we all paid for it.",
      runeguard: "Four graves round him, each in its owner's colour, their places drawn anew each pull. The writ, the crown in close, then each to their own before it closes; never another's. I lay in mine as one reads a draft: carefully, and aloud.",
      gorgemaw: "The King set four graves, one per guest, each in their own colour, and shuffled the seats each time. After his crown we each took our own place. Sit in another's and you're served a beating. Well presented.",
      oathbroker: "Each of the undersigned is allotted a grave in his own colour, the allotment drawn anew each hearing. Terms: be in your own when it closes. Lying in another's is billed as well, and an empty grave raises the dead on us all.",
      ironpalm: "He gave each of us a grave, in our own colour. The places change each time. In close for his crown. Then out, each to our own. I lay in mine as a man lies in a bed.",
      ranger: "Four graves, each our colour. They move around between pulls, so look at the colours. Crown in close, then out, plenty of time. Never step in another's.",
      shadowblade: "The mark dug us each a grave in our own colour, in a new arrangement every time. After the crown I slipped out to mine, quietly. Lie in someone else's and it's rather rude of you, and it hurts.",
      pyromancer: "He dug me a grave! In my own colour, which was thoughtful, though they shuffle between pulls. In close for his crown, then out to my own before it closed. Someone else's would have hurt. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Four graves, our colours, shuffled each pull. Crown first, in close. Then out to mine. Someone stood in the wrong one and the dead rose for us all. Back to the middle after. Never lie in the wrong plot!",
      driftblade: "He dug four graves in our colours, in a new order every pull, and I circled him through the writ and the crown and then swooped out to mine and lay still in it, which I hate, but only for a breath.",
      marionettist: "The star lays out four graves, each lit in its player's colour, the blocking changed every night. The cast comes in close for his crown, then exits each to their own grave. (The Doll has no grave. A relief.)",
      cleric: "He opened a grave for each of us, in our own colours, and which is whose is drawn fresh each time, so I read the colours. After his crown we each went to our own. Our young archer lay in mine once, and was struck for it. Dawn keep her.",
      grovekeeper: "Four graves, dug in our own colours, in different beds each season, dear. My lot came in close for his crown, then each went out to their own and lay still. An empty bed, and the dead come up for us all.",
      chronomancer: "The graves open in our colours, the places drawn anew each pull, and close a while later, after the writ and the crown. I walked out to mine in good time, plenty of beats to spare.",
      alembist: "Four graves, one per hero, each labelled in their colour, the labels reshuffled each pull. In close for the crown, then everyone to their own before it shuts. An empty grave raises the dead on us all. Colour-code the corks. Please.",
      threadweaver: "He dug a grave for each of us, each in its own colour, the places drawn fresh each pull. After the crown each went to their own. One wandered into the wrong one. A sloppy seam.",
    } },
    'Writ of Execution': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Chained, every man of us, and the writ read out. My line went into a corner between the graves, and I stepped off it. It burns on, so aim with care; a line is a wall. One writ is torn up each pull, and I never learned whose.",
      gloomsworn: "A chain on each of us, the writ read aloud, and a beam of grave-light through every one, burning on after. Mine I turned into a corner between the graves and stepped away. One writ was struck from the record, and which one changes each time.",
      thresher: "Chains on each of us and a line of grave-light through every chain. I aimed mine at a far corner between the graves, a lane nobody needs to cross, and stood off it. It keeps burning, so a badly aimed line walls somebody out.",
      bloodreaver: "Chains on all of us, then a line out through each! Take your own, keep it off the others. They stay burning, so aim between the graves. One of us was pardoned, a different one each pull, lucky thing, and missed a good scar.",
      runeguard: "A chain to each of us, one hanging broken. Each points their line out between the graves, apart from the rest, and steps off; the lines burn on until the graves close. The broken chain is, I think, the part the court is sorry for.",
      gorgemaw: "The King chained his guests and sent a course of grave-light down each chain. Aim yours toward a corner, between the graves, for the line stays hot on the table. One guest's writ was torn up: no serving. Underseasoned.",
      oathbroker: "The writ names each of the undersigned, save one, whose is torn in two. Terms: point yours between the graves and away from the others, then step aside. The line keeps burning, and one aimed at a grave closes the way.",
      ironpalm: "He chained us all and read the writ. A line out through each of us. I sent mine between the graves, then stepped away. It kept burning. I let it speak to the empty wall.",
      ranger: "Chain on each of us; a line out through each. Mine to the corner, between graves, apart from the rest, then step off. It burns after. Aimed at a grave, it walls you out.",
      shadowblade: "The mark chained us all and read out a line for each. I sent mine into a corner between the graves and stepped out of it. The lines stay burning, so a careless aim bars someone's way home. Nobody noticed me do it.",
      pyromancer: "He read a writ and a line of grave-fire ran out through each of us! I aimed mine at a corner, between the graves, and hopped aside. It kept burning, which I admired. It is not often another fire outstays me.",
      thermist: "Chains, a line each. Point it between the graves. Step off. It kept burning on the floor, a spoke out of him, and I nearly ran through it to my grave. Too far. Reading: do not run through spokes.",
      driftblade: "Every one of us chained, a line out through each, so I swept mine into a corner between the graves and stepped off, and later threaded the burning spokes out to my grave, as light as a bird.",
      marionettist: "The leading man reads a writ: a line from him through each player. The cast points theirs to the corners, between the graves, and steps aside; the lines burn on as scenery. (One writ is torn up. Not mine.)",
      cleric: "He chained us all, but my writ was torn, so I was free to stand where I could reach everyone. The others aimed their lines between the graves and stepped aside. Thank the light for a torn page.",
      grovekeeper: "My writ was torn, so I stood clear of the others' lines and kept mending. They each sent their line toward a corner, between the graves, like rows planted so the paths stay open.",
      chronomancer: "Pardoned, this time, which was last time: my writ was torn. The others aimed theirs between the graves and stepped off as it fired. The lines burn on until the graves close. I might have known.",
      alembist: "The Writ: a line through each of us that stays burning. Aim it between the graves, away from the others, and step off. My own writ came torn, so I brewed where I liked. Lucky. Whoever draws the short card, I salute you.",
      threadweaver: "My writ was torn, so I tended my stitches from wherever I liked. Each sent their line out to a corner, between the graves, so the burning lines left every path open.",
    } },
  },
};
